17592186426308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186611564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186611582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186625576} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186636426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186661570}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cofactor Method To Solve for an Unknown Quantity"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059756 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059565} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050163}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186611622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186625610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186625613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186636424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186636513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657508}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060933 :tutorial/content "We can calculate the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ using the following five-step algorithm.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the determinant $\\det(A)$ of the matrix.\n\n**Step 2:** Form the **matrix $M$ of the minors** of $A,$ where each entry $a_{ij}$ of $A$ is replaced by its minor $M_{ij}\\mathbin{:}$\n   $$\nM = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}{M}_{11} & {M}_{12} & {M}_{13} \\\\ {M}_{21} & {M}_{22} & {M}_{23} \\\\ {M}_{31} & {M}_{32} & {M}_{33}\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the **matrix $C$ of cofactors** by changing the sign of some entries of the matrix of the minors according to the **rule of alternating signs**:\n   \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}+ & - & + \\\\ - & + & - \\\\ + & - & +\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFor each entry of $M$, we change the sign if the corresponding sign in the pattern is \"$-$\". Otherwise, if the corresponding sign in the pattern is \"$+$\", then we don't change the entry.\n\nSo, the matrix of cofactors is given by\n\n$$\nC = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}{M}_{11} & - {M}_{12} & {M}_{13} \\\\ - {M}_{21} & {M}_{22} & - {M}_{23} \\\\ {M}_{31} & - {M}_{32} & {M}_{33}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors, $C^T.$\n\n$$\nC^T = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}{M}_{11} & - {M}_{21} & {M}_{31} \\\\ - {M}_{12} & {M}_{22} & - {M}_{32} \\\\ {M}_{13} & - {M}_{23} & {M}_{33}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix:\n   \n\n$$\nA^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{\\det ( A)}C^{T}\n$$\n\nIn other words, we divide each entry of the transpose of the matrix of cofactors by the determinant of the original matrix (which we computed in step 1).\n\nLet's see this in practice, in the next example." :tutorial/id #uuid "9c7ba5cd-b4b2-595b-bbad-dfbdac248280" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3051 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060934 :tutorial/content "We make the matrix $C$ of cofactors by changing the sign of alternate entries of the matrix $M$ of the minors. So, each entry $c_{ij}$ of $C$ equals\n\n$$\nc_{ij} = (-1)^{i+j} M_{ij},\n$$\n\nwhere $M_{ij}$ is the minor of the entry $a_{ij}$ in the matrix $A.$\n\nWith this and the formula for the inverse, we can write the formula for each entry $a'_{ij}$ of the inverse matrix $A^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\na'_{ij} = \\dfrac{1}{\\det(A)}c^t_{ij}\n        = \\dfrac{1}{\\det(A)} c_{ji}\n$$\n\nHere, we used the fact that the entry $c^t_{ij}$ of $C^T$ is equal to the entry $c_{ji}$ of $C$ by the definition of the transpose." :tutorial/id #uuid "835c9ab0-dbce-5297-9a57-3978df178fa3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3052 :tutorial/title "Calculating Individual Entries of the Matrix of Cofactors"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087701 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087508} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bea23649-e835-540a-b238-7b89ed330cd3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049125}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186638274}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087702 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087509} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c6056ee-15f3-5b5c-8a14-173ea1da1381" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600404}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087703 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087510} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e64e0c3-f8f5-5c38-ae75-1aed92e0ac01" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048774}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299503} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600357}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Variable in a Singular Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088895 :tutorial/content "Given a $2 \\times 2$ matrix\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A =\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nthe **inverse** of $A$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9694c9ae-0d23-54a2-a458-b9c2262e4fba" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3045 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088896 :tutorial/content "Remember that the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix <div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div> is given by\n\n$$\nA^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{\\det(A)}\n\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $\\det(A)=0,$ then $A^{-1}$ doesn't exist since we can't divide by zero. In fact, we have the following statement:\n\n> $A^{-1}$ exists if and only if $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nA matrix that has an inverse is called **invertible**. Otherwise, the matrix doesn't have an inverse and is called **non-invertible**. A square matrix that is non-invertible is called **singular**." :tutorial/id #uuid "da6abb7f-408d-5c0d-bd11-70b1723463a3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3046 :tutorial/title "Invertible and Singular Matrices"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088897 :tutorial/content "Given a matrix $A = \n\t\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix},$\nwe have been using the following formula to compute the inverse:\n\n$$\nA^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{ad-bc}\n\t\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo check that this formula is correct for a general matrix $A$, we need to verify that\n\n$$\nAA^{-1} = A^{-1}A = I_2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $A^{-1}A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}\\cdot A & =\\frac{1}{ad-bc}\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{ad-bc}\\begin{bmatrix}ad-bc & 0 \\\\ 0 & -bc+ad\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute $A A^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot {A}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{ad-bc}\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{ad-bc}\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{ad-bc}\\begin{bmatrix}ad-bc & 0 \\\\ 0 & -bc+ad\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have verified that\n\n$$\nAA^{-1} = A^{-1}A = I_2,\n$$\n\nand we can be sure that the given formula is the correct formula for $A^{-1}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bb6e0c6d-f129-56b9-9bed-f9e6245197b9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3047 :tutorial/title "Verifying the Formula"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119781 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119595} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecdc6a5a-5aba-5547-97aa-db3cd2e90ae6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048146}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393091} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186666097}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Minor in a NxN Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119782 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119596} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58148550-e41f-5df0-80dc-e783619cf87d" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392455} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186666069}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Cofactor in a NxN Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119783 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119597} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "055b3809-f9aa-5dcc-8c1a-b0eb71020d7e" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643139}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a NxN Matrix Using Cofactor Expansion"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121025 :tutorial/content "The minor of the entry $a_{ij}$ in a $n\\times n$ matrix is the determinant that we get when the rows and columns containing $a_{ij}$ are removed.\n\nFor example, suppose that we wish to find the minor of $a_{32}$ in the following matrix:\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 5 & 12 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nTo get the minor of $a_{\\color{red}{3}\\color{blue}{2}}$, we remove the $\\color{red}3$rd row and $\\color{blue}2$nd column:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & ∗ & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & ∗ & 5 & 12 \\\\ ∗ & -2 & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ -5 & ∗ & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of $a_{32}$, denoted $M_{32}$, is \n\n$$\nM_{32}=\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 12 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe can now calculate the value of $M_{32}$ by evaluating the determinant using previously discussed methods." :tutorial/id #uuid "c271b45c-6e89-58a5-b33f-72802abba7b7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3020 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121059 :tutorial/content "One way to find the determinant of a $3\\!\\times\\! 3$ matrix $A$ is to take the alternating sum of the entries of the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = {\\color{red}a_{11}}{\\color{blue}M_{11}} - {\\color{red}a_{12}}{\\color{blue}M_{12}} + {\\color{red}a_{13}}{\\color{blue}M_{13}}\n$$\n\nRemember that we had to change the sign of the second product. When we multiply a minor of an entry by the respective sign in the formula like this, we get the cofactor of the entry. The cofactors in the above formula were ${\\color{blue}M_{11}},$ $-{\\color{blue}M_{12}},$ and ${\\color{blue}M_{13}}.$\n\nUsing this idea, we can write the formula for the determinant of a $3\\!\\times\\! 3$ matrix $A$ in terms of cofactors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = {\\color{red}a_{11}}{\\color{blue}C_{11}} + {\\color{red}a_{12}}{\\color{blue}C_{12}} + {\\color{red}a_{13}}{\\color{blue}C_{13}}\n$$\n\nThis is called the **cofactor expansion** of the determinant across the first row.\n\nIn fact, we can use a similar method of multiplying the entries of the top row of the matrix by their respective cofactors to find the determinant for any $n\\!\\times\\! n$ matrix $A$ when $n \\ge 3$. The formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{1j}{C}_{1j} \\\\  & ={a}_{11}{C}_{11}+{a}_{12}{C}_{12}+⋯+{a}_{1n}{C}_{1n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see how to use this formula in the next example." :tutorial/id #uuid "d83d34fa-1d87-5510-bfd8-8673b2bd79cf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3801 :tutorial/title "Calculating Determinants Using Cofactors"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121060 :tutorial/content "In general, for any $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ the **cofactor** $C_{ij}$ of the entry $\\color{red}a_{ij}$ is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}C_{ij}}=(-1)^{i+j}{\\color{blue}M_{ij}}.\n$$\n\nCofactors are a useful tool in helping us to compute determinants of larger matrices. Before we learn how, let's get some practice at computing cofactors." :tutorial/id #uuid "6c2b7774-d437-5cf9-812f-4d6ad8dc9bf3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3802 :tutorial/title "Calculating a Cofactor of a NxN Matrix"}]}
